//@version=6
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  XAU LIQUIDITY ENGINE  v2
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  A liquidity engine for XAUUSD: swing highs and lows as BSL / SSL, fair
//  value gaps with CE, and resting limit orders at untouched levels with a
//  structural stop and a higher-timeframe target. Every algorithm here is a
//  public, well-documented one, implemented from scratch.
//
//  DESIGN NOTES
//
//    The orange line is EMA 21, not EMA 27.
//      "Show EMA 21" is the only EMA enabled, and its colour is #FF9800.
//      EMA 9, 20 and 50 all exist in the script and are all switched off.
//
//    The purple line is VWAP, not a slow EMA. Colour #9C27B0.
//      This finally explains the thing v1 could not: the purple line appears
//      only in the recent part of the chart because VWAP anchors to the session
//      and restarts. A 55-period EMA would have run the full width.
//
//    27 / 1.6 / 55 / 2 are not moving averages at all.
//      They are "Fast Period / Fast Range / Slow Period / Slow Range" - the
//      parameters of a DUAL RANGE FILTER. That is where the signals come from.
//
//    The shaded boxes are FAIR VALUE GAPS, not order blocks.
//      With "use candle Bodies" ON, so they are body-to-body gaps. The dotted
//      line through each one is CE - consequent encroachment, the 50% level.
//      Teal is configured for DOWN gaps and dull red for UP gaps, which is the
//      reverse of the usual convention and worth knowing before it confuses you.
//
//    What v1 DID get right: signals strictly alternate. The mechanism is the
//    range filter's CondIni state variable, which only lets a long through when
//    the previous state was short. v1 guessed an ATR envelope around an MA -
//    right in spirit, wrong in mechanism.
//
//  EVERY NUMBER IN THE STATUS LINE, DECODED
//    5           Base Swing Strength          10,000,000  Delete After (Bars)
//    0.5         Major Level Threshold %      75          Traded-Through Opacity
//    Solid       Line Style                   close       Range filter source
//    2           Line Width                   27 / 1.6    Fast Period / Range
//    Right       Label Placement              55 / 2      Slow Period / Range
//    365         days lookback                dot         CE line style
//    1 1 2 1 2   EMA20 / EMA9 / EMA21 / EMA50 / VWAP line widths
//
//  STILL NOT KNOWN
//    Whether the fast and slow filters must AGREE to fire, or whether one of
//    them drives signals alone. Requiring agreement is the best explanation for
//    why so few signals appear, so it is the default - but it is a deduction,
//    not a reading. "Signal source" switches it.
//
//  NOW MEASURED — AND THE SIGNALS HAVE NO EDGE
//    Backtested on 2M, 3M and 5M (January, resampled from M1) and 5M
//    (June-August). Full method and tables in docs/FINDINGS_range_filter.md.
//
//      Range filter  1,131 trades   +0.002R   95% CI [-0.079, +0.084]   P(no edge) 49%
//      Mean reversion  213 trades   +0.263R   95% CI [+0.071, +0.460]   P(no edge)  0.3%
//
//    That range-filter line uses the CALIBRATED rule, so it is a measurement of
//    the real indicator's markers and not of my approximation to them.
//
//    Direction accuracy is 46-56% at every horizon tested - a coin. Fourteen
//    attempts to fix it (target 1R/1.5R/2R/3R, wider and tighter stops, session
//    filter, EMA200 alignment, band filter, fast-only, and INVERTING every
//    signal) produced not one configuration positive in both periods. Fading the
//    signals loses money too, which is what genuinely no information looks like.
//
//    997 trades is a well-powered null, not a shortage of data.
//
//    So: keep this script for its MAP - the liquidity levels, the gaps and CE,
//    EMA 21 and VWAP are useful furniture. For entries set "Signal engine" to
//    Mean reversion, and prefer 5M (+0.497R), then 3M (+0.375R). 2M measured
//    +0.135R on one sample of 47 trades and is not established.
//
//    Two incidental findings, in case you tune this yourself:
//      "Both must agree" is nearly identical to "slow only" - the slow filter is
//      the binding constraint, so the question the settings dialog could not
//      answer turns out not to matter much.
//      A "price beyond the band" rule is a NO-OP by construction: when the
//      filter moves it moves to exactly price - range, so the comparison is an
//      equality and never a strict inequality.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

indicator("XAU Liquidity Engine", "XAU-LE", overlay = true,
     max_boxes_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500,
     max_bars_back = 5000)

// Pine addresses a drawing's bar-index coordinate as an offset from the current
// bar, and that offset must sit inside the historical buffer. A liquidity level
// can outlive that buffer: the per-side FIFO cap only evicts when a NEW level is
// pushed, and f_absorb merges a nearby swing into an existing level WITHOUT
// refreshing its birth bar. So in a tight range where every swing gets absorbed,
// nothing is pushed, nothing is evicted, and one level ages without limit until
// its birth bar falls off the end - which is the runtime error
//   "The requested historical offset (356) is beyond the historical buffer's
//    limit (355)"
// Raising the buffer to 5000 buys room; clamping every backward reference to
// MAX_BACK below guarantees it can never happen again on a longer chart.
MAX_BACK = 5000

gENG = "① Signal engine"
gRF  = "② Range filter  ·  the signal source"
gLIQ = "③ Liquidity levels  ·  BSL / SSL / REQH / REQL"
gFVG = "④ Fair value gaps  ·  with CE"
gMA  = "⑤ Moving averages & VWAP"
gMR  = "⑥ Mean reversion  ·  your measured engine"

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ① ENGINE
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
engine = input.string("Range filter", "Signal engine", group = gENG,
     options = ["Range filter", "Mean reversion", "Compare both"],
     tooltip = "Range filter - the reference mechanism, rebuilt. Measured at " +
               "-0.045R over 997 trades: no edge. Kept because replicating it " +
               "was the point, not because it works.\n" +
               "Mean reversion - +0.263R over 213 trades, P(no edge) 0.3%. This " +
               "is the one to trade.\n" +
               "Compare both - each in its own style, on one chart.")
useRF = engine == "Range filter"  or engine == "Compare both"
useMR = engine == "Mean reversion" or engine == "Compare both"

showLbl  = input.bool(true, "Signal labels", group = gENG, inline = "mk")
showBand = input.bool(true, "Vertical band", group = gENG, inline = "mk")
maxSigLbl = input.int(200, "Signal labels kept", minval = 20, maxval = 400, group = gENG,
     tooltip = "TradingView destroys the OLDEST labels past 500, which would eat " +
               "the level labels. Keeping our own budget means signals age out " +
               "instead of levels.")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ② DUAL RANGE FILTER
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  A range filter is a step-follower: it moves only when price travels further
//  than a smoothed measure of recent range, and otherwise holds flat. Because it
//  holds, it does not whipsaw the way a moving average does - which is what makes
//  its signals sparse enough to match what the chart shows.
rfSrc   = input.source(close, "Source", group = gRF)
fastPer = input.int(38,  "Fast period", minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = gRF, inline = "f")
fastRng = input.float(1.8, "range", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = gRF, inline = "f")
slowPer = input.int(55,  "Slow period", minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = gRF, inline = "s")
slowRng = input.float(2.0, "range", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = gRF, inline = "s")
rfMode  = input.string("Fast only", "Signal source", group = gRF,
     options = ["Both must agree", "Fast only", "Slow only"],
     tooltip = "Settled by calibration, not deduction. A single filter at 38/1.8 " +
               "reproduces 159 of the reference 169 markers on 5M (F1 0.96) and " +
               "152 of 170 on 3M. Requiring both to agree reproduces far fewer.")
minAdx  = input.float(20.0, "Minimum ADX to accept a signal", minval = 0, step = 1, group = gRF,
     tooltip = "0 = the reference unfiltered behaviour.\n\n" +
               "20 removes 41% of signals - the low-trend-strength ones, where a " +
               "trend-follower has no business firing - and nudges measured " +
               "expectancy from -0.031R to +0.053R over 670 trades.\n\n" +
               "Be clear about what that is worth: the interval still contains " +
               "zero (P(no edge) 22%). It is here because paying a spread on 41% " +
               "fewer trades is worth having, NOT because it is proven profitable. " +
               "docs/FINDINGS_signal_improvement.md has the working.")
showBands = input.bool(false, "Draw the fast filter and its band", group = gRF)

// Smoothed range: an EMA of bar-to-bar movement, smoothed again over a window
// twice as long, then scaled. The double smoothing is what stops the filter
// reacting to a single wide bar.
f_smoothrng(float x, simple int t, float m) =>
    wper  = t * 2 - 1
    avrng = ta.ema(math.abs(x - x[1]), t)
    ta.ema(avrng, wper) * m

// The filter itself. It only ever moves toward price, and only by the amount
// price exceeded the range by - so it ratchets and never retraces.
f_rngfilt(float x, float r) =>
    var float f = na
    prev = na(f) ? x : f
    out  = x > prev ? (x - r < prev ? prev : x - r) : (x + r > prev ? prev : x + r)
    f := out
    out

// How many consecutive bars the filter has been rising or falling. A signal
// needs the filter to be moving its way, not merely to be on the right side.
f_dir(float filt) =>
    var float up = 0.0
    var float dn = 0.0
    up := filt > filt[1] ? up + 1 : filt < filt[1] ? 0.0 : up
    dn := filt < filt[1] ? dn + 1 : filt > filt[1] ? 0.0 : dn
    [up, dn]

// THE ALTERNATION. `ini` remembers which side fired last, and a long is only
// emitted when the previous state was short. This single variable is why eight
// consecutive signals on the reference data alternate perfectly, and it is the
// part of the mechanism the reference data alone made visible.
f_state(bool longCond, bool shortCond) =>
    var int ini = 0
    lc = longCond  and ini == -1
    sc = shortCond and ini == 1
    ini := longCond ? 1 : shortCond ? -1 : ini
    [lc, sc]

fRng  = f_smoothrng(rfSrc, fastPer, fastRng)
fFilt = f_rngfilt(rfSrc, fRng)
[fUp, fDn] = f_dir(fFilt)

sRng  = f_smoothrng(rfSrc, slowPer, slowRng)
sFilt = f_rngfilt(rfSrc, sRng)
[sUp, sDn] = f_dir(sFilt)

fLongRaw  = rfSrc > fFilt and fUp > 0
fShortRaw = rfSrc < fFilt and fDn > 0
sLongRaw  = rfSrc > sFilt and sUp > 0
sShortRaw = rfSrc < sFilt and sDn > 0

longRaw  = rfMode == "Fast only" ? fLongRaw  : rfMode == "Slow only" ? sLongRaw  : fLongRaw and sLongRaw
shortRaw = rfMode == "Fast only" ? fShortRaw : rfMode == "Slow only" ? sShortRaw : fShortRaw and sShortRaw

[rfLongSig, rfShortSig] = f_state(longRaw, shortRaw)

// The ADX gate sits AFTER the alternation state, not before it. Gating the raw
// conditions instead would let the state flip on a bar whose signal was then
// suppressed, and the next opposite signal would be silently swallowed - the
// markers would stop alternating and start disappearing in pairs.
[diPlusV, diMinusV, adxVal] = ta.dmi(14, 14)
adxOk = minAdx <= 0 or adxVal >= minAdx
rfLong  = useRF and rfLongSig  and adxOk
rfShort = useRF and rfShortSig and adxOk

plot(showBands ? fFilt : na, "Range filter", color.new(#787B86, 20), 2)
plot(showBands ? fFilt + fRng : na, "Upper band", color.new(#26A69A, 60), 1)
plot(showBands ? fFilt - fRng : na, "Lower band", color.new(#EF5350, 60), 1)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ⑤ MOVING AVERAGES & VWAP   (declared before the drawing code that needs ATR)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
showE9  = input.bool(false, "EMA 9",  group = gMA, inline = "e1")
showE20 = input.bool(false, "EMA 20", group = gMA, inline = "e1")
showE21 = input.bool(true,  "EMA 21", group = gMA, inline = "e2")
showE50 = input.bool(false, "EMA 50", group = gMA, inline = "e2")
showVwap = input.bool(true, "VWAP",   group = gMA)
colE9   = input.color(#FDD835, "", group = gMA, inline = "e1")
colE20  = input.color(#2962FF, "", group = gMA, inline = "e1")
colE21  = input.color(#FF9800, "", group = gMA, inline = "e2")
colE50  = input.color(#F23645, "", group = gMA, inline = "e2")
colVwap = input.color(#9C27B0, "", group = gMA)
atrLen  = input.int(14, "ATR length (sizing for labels & zones)", minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = gMA)

ema9  = ta.ema(close, 9)
ema20 = ta.ema(close, 20)
ema21 = ta.ema(close, 21)
ema50 = ta.ema(close, 50)
vwap  = ta.vwap(hlc3)
atr   = ta.atr(atrLen)

plot(showE9  ? ema9  : na, "EMA 9",  colE9,  1)
plot(showE20 ? ema20 : na, "EMA 20", colE20, 1)
plot(showE21 ? ema21 : na, "EMA 21", colE21, 2)
plot(showE50 ? ema50 : na, "EMA 50", colE50, 1)
plot(showVwap ? vwap : na, "VWAP",   colVwap, 2)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ③ LIQUIDITY LEVELS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
swingLen  = input.int(5, "Base swing strength", minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "A swing needs this many bars either side, so a level is only known " +
               "this many bars AFTER it formed. Scrolling back they look perfectly " +
               "placed; live they arrive late. Unavoidable, not a bug.")
majorPct  = input.float(0.5, "Major level threshold %", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "Swing size, as a percentage of price, above which a level is " +
               "labelled 'Major'. Confirmed from the reference 1h chart, which " +
               "shows 'Major BSL' alongside plain 'BSL' - so this classifies a " +
               "level rather than suppressing it.")
majorOnly = input.bool(false, "Draw only major levels", group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "Off matches the reference. On is a decluttering switch.")
eqTolATR  = input.float(0.35, "Relatively-equal tolerance (× ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "A later swing landing within this of an existing level makes the " +
               "pair 'relatively equal' - REQH / REQL - which holds far more " +
               "resting orders than a single swing does.")
showPlan  = input.bool(true, "Project the trade plan onto live levels", group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "Draws where the limit order, stop and target would go BEFORE " +
               "price arrives - the level already exists, so this is arithmetic, " +
               "not a forecast.\n\n" +
               "It exists because a sweep-and-reclaim signal cannot fire until " +
               "the reclaim candle CLOSES, by which time the first part of the " +
               "move is gone. A resting limit gets you in during the sweep.\n\n" +
               "Measured on 5M gold against the confirmation entry, on identical " +
               "levels, stops and targets: +0.523R vs +0.222R, a $5.22 median " +
               "stop vs $6.25, 30 minutes in the trade vs 60, and less than half " +
               "the drawdown. 93% of the surrounding parameter grid was positive " +
               "in both test periods, against a ~25% noise baseline.\n\n" +
               "Read docs/FINDINGS_anticipation.md first - it also documents the " +
               "much larger number this replaced, which was an artifact.")
planK     = input.float(0.25, "Limit offset beyond level (× ATR)", minval = 0, step = 0.05, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "How far PAST the level to rest the order. Zero - buying exactly " +
               "at the level - measured NEGATIVE. The overshoot is the point: " +
               "you want to be filled by the stop-run, not by the level.")
planFloor = input.float(0.50, "Minimum stop distance (× ATR)", minval = 0.1, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "The stop goes at the last swing beyond the level, but when that " +
               "swing sits almost on top of the fill the stop is not placeable - " +
               "spread alone would take it out. This pushes it out to a real " +
               "distance.\n\n" +
               "Removing this floor is what produced the +0.97R figure an earlier " +
               "version of this script advertised. That number was risk divided " +
               "by nearly zero, not profit.")
planMaxR  = input.float(6.0, "Skip if target further than (× risk)", minval = 1.0, maxval = 20.0, step = 0.5, group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "If the higher-timeframe objective is further away than this, the " +
               "trade needs a move too large to count on, and is skipped. " +
               "Tightening to 4R made results worse; 8R held up.")
planHTF   = input.timeframe("60", "Target timeframe", group = gLIQ,
     tooltip = "The target is the extreme of the last few CLOSED bars of this " +
               "timeframe: lower-timeframe entry, higher-timeframe objective.")
planSpan  = input.int(6, "Target lookback (HTF bars)", minval = 2, maxval = 40, group = gLIQ)
showLiq   = input.bool(true, "Show levels", group = gLIQ)
liqWidth  = input.int(2, "Line width", minval = 1, maxval = 4, group = gLIQ)
keepTraded = input.bool(false, "Keep traded-through levels", group = gLIQ)
tradedOpac = input.int(75, "Traded-through opacity %", minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = gLIQ)
maxLevels = input.int(8, "Max live levels per side", minval = 1, maxval = 25, group = gLIQ)
colBuy    = input.color(#089944, "Buyside",  group = gLIQ, inline = "lc")
colSell   = input.color(#E91E63, "Sellside", group = gLIQ, inline = "lc")

COL_NONE = color.new(color.black, 100)

type Level
    float price
    int   born
    int   died
    int   touches
    bool  isBuy
    bool  isMajor
    bool  traded
    line  ln
    label lb
    line  pEnt
    line  pSl
    line  pTp
    label pLb

var array<Level> levels = array.new<Level>()

// Recent swings, kept as raw prices so the plan can find its stop. These are
// deliberately NOT the Level array: levels get absorbed, evicted and deleted
// when they are traded through, and a stop needs the swing that is actually
// still on the chart underneath the entry, whether or not it is drawn.
var array<float> pvHi = array.new<float>()
var array<float> pvLo = array.new<float>()
PV_KEEP = 40

// The higher-timeframe objective: the extreme of the last planSpan CLOSED bars
// of planHTF. [1] and lookahead_off together are what stop this from reading a
// bar that has not finished forming - without both, every historical target on
// the chart would be one the live chart could never have known.
htfHi = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, planHTF, ta.highest(high, planSpan)[1],
     lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
htfLo = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, planHTF, ta.lowest(low, planSpan)[1],
     lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

// Nearest swing beyond the level - the market's own invalidation point, rather
// than a multiple of ATR chosen to make a backtest look good.
//
// isHigh is the Level's isBuy flag, and the name is worth being pedantic about:
// isBuy = true means the level IS a swing high, so it is BSL - the stops of
// sellers, resting above it. Sweeping it is a SELL setup, not a buy. Getting
// this backwards silently inverts every trade the script draws.
//
// For that sell, the stop is the LOWEST swing high still above the level:
// anything higher is further away than the idea requires.
f_structStop(float lvl, bool isHigh) =>
    float best = na
    arr = isHigh ? pvHi : pvLo
    if array.size(arr) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(arr) - 1
            v = array.get(arr, i)
            if isHigh
                if v > lvl and (na(best) or v < best)
                    best := v
            else
                if v < lvl and (na(best) or v > best)
                    best := v
    best

// The 1h chart shows levels labelled "Major BSL", which settles what the 0.5%
// threshold actually does: it CLASSIFIES a level, it does not filter it out.
// Both plain and Major levels are drawn. REQH/REQL is a separate axis - it means
// a later swing landed on top of an existing one, so the pool holds more stops.
f_levelText(Level L) =>
    stem = L.touches >= 2 ? (L.isBuy ? "REQH" : "REQL") : (L.isBuy ? "BSL" : "SSL")
    (L.isMajor ? "Major " : "") + stem

f_drawLevel(Level L) =>
    base = L.isBuy ? colBuy : colSell
    col  = L.traded ? color.new(base, tradedOpac) : base
    // A level nobody has taken keeps projecting. Once traded through it freezes
    // at the bar it died on - stretching it to the right edge would keep saying
    // the level still matters, which is precisely what taking it out disproved.
    ext  = L.traded ? extend.none : extend.right
    // Never address a bar older than the buffer allows. For a live level the
    // line carries extend.right, so a clamped left anchor is purely cosmetic.
    // A traded level uses extend.none, so if BOTH ends fell past the clamp it
    // would collapse to zero length - unreachable unless "keep traded levels"
    // is on AND the level is 4900+ bars old, but worth not pretending otherwise.
    oldest = bar_index - (MAX_BACK - 100)
    xStart = math.max(L.born, oldest)
    xEnd   = math.max(L.traded ? L.died : bar_index, oldest)
    line.set_xy1(L.ln, xStart, L.price)
    line.set_xy2(L.ln, xEnd, L.price)
    line.set_color(L.ln, showLiq ? col : COL_NONE)
    line.set_extend(L.ln, ext)
    line.set_width(L.ln, (L.isMajor or L.touches >= 2) ? liqWidth : math.max(liqWidth - 1, 1))
    label.set_xy(L.lb, L.traded ? xEnd : bar_index + 6, L.price)
    label.set_text(L.lb, f_levelText(L) + (L.touches > 1 ? " ×" + str.tostring(L.touches) : ""))
    label.set_textcolor(L.lb, showLiq ? col : COL_NONE)

    // The plan only means anything while the level is still untouched. Once it
    // has been traded through there is nothing left to rest an order against.
    // A swing HIGH is buyside liquidity, so taking it is a SELL. The entry rests
    // BEYOND the level, never at it - buying exactly at the level measured
    // negative, because the fill you want is the one the stop-run gives you.
    short = L.isBuy
    ent   = short ? L.price + planK * atr : L.price - planK * atr
    // Structure first, ATR only as the fallback when no swing qualifies.
    sv    = f_structStop(L.price, L.isBuy)
    rawSl = na(sv) ? (short ? ent + planFloor * atr : ent - planFloor * atr)
     : (short ? sv + 0.10 * atr : sv - 0.10 * atr)
    // Floor it, or spread alone takes the trade out.
    minD  = planFloor * atr
    sl    = short ? math.max(rawSl, ent + minD) : math.min(rawSl, ent - minD)
    tp    = short ? htfLo : htfHi
    risk  = math.abs(ent - sl)
    // Three ways the plan is not a plan: no target yet, the target is the wrong
    // side of the entry, or reaching it needs a move too big to count on.
    ok    = risk > 0 and not na(tp) and (short ? tp < ent : tp > ent)
     and math.abs(tp - ent) / risk <= planMaxR and risk <= 2.5 * atr
    plan  = showPlan and not L.traded and ok
    x0    = bar_index
    pExt  = plan ? extend.right : extend.none
    // Pine takes one statement per line - no comma-chaining - and unrolling
    // beats allocating a throwaway array on every level, every repaint.
    line.set_xy1(L.pEnt, x0, ent)
    line.set_xy2(L.pEnt, x0 + 3, ent)
    line.set_extend(L.pEnt, pExt)
    line.set_xy1(L.pSl, x0, sl)
    line.set_xy2(L.pSl, x0 + 3, sl)
    line.set_extend(L.pSl, pExt)
    // tp is na until enough higher-timeframe bars have closed; anchoring the
    // line to ent in that case keeps it a zero-length invisible stub rather
    // than letting a na coordinate quietly rescale the pane.
    tpD = na(tp) ? ent : tp
    line.set_xy1(L.pTp, x0, tpD)
    line.set_xy2(L.pTp, x0 + 3, tpD)
    line.set_extend(L.pTp, pExt)
    line.set_color(L.pEnt, plan ? color.new(col, 20) : COL_NONE)
    line.set_color(L.pSl, plan ? color.new(colSell, 30) : COL_NONE)
    line.set_color(L.pTp, plan ? color.new(colBuy, 30) : COL_NONE)
    label.set_xy(L.pLb, bar_index + 6, ent)
    label.set_text(L.pLb, plan ? (short ? "SELL LIMIT " : "BUY LIMIT ") +
         str.tostring(ent, format.mintick) + "   SL " + str.tostring(sl, format.mintick) +
         " ($" + str.tostring(risk, "#.##") + ")   TP " + str.tostring(tpD, format.mintick) +
         "  " + str.tostring(math.abs(tpD - ent) / risk, "#.#") + "R" : "")
    label.set_textcolor(L.pLb, plan ? col : COL_NONE)

f_killLevel(Level L) =>
    line.delete(L.ln)
    label.delete(L.lb)
    line.delete(L.pEnt)
    line.delete(L.pSl)
    line.delete(L.pTp)
    label.delete(L.pLb)

ph = ta.pivothigh(high, swingLen, swingLen)
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,  swingLen, swingLen)
tol = eqTolATR * atr

// The swing is confirmed swingLen bars after it formed, so price has already had
// swingLen bars to run through it. Computed unconditionally because ta.* must be.
hiLag = ta.highest(high, swingLen)
loLag = ta.lowest(low,   swingLen)

// Feed the plan's stop-finder. Bounded and oldest-first out, so a long session
// cannot grow these without limit and a stop can never be anchored to a swing
// that scrolled off the chart hours ago.
if not na(ph)
    array.push(pvHi, ph)
    if array.size(pvHi) > PV_KEEP
        array.shift(pvHi)
if not na(pl)
    array.push(pvLo, pl)
    if array.size(pvLo) > PV_KEEP
        array.shift(pvLo)

f_absorb(float lvl, bool isBuy) =>
    // Fold a new swing into an existing level rather than spawning a rival one.
    // Without this a second high one tick above the first creates a competing
    // level instead of confirming the pair, and equal highs can never form.
    // Newest-first with an early exit, so a level cannot ratchet across the chart.
    bool merged = false
    if array.size(levels) > 0
        for i = array.size(levels) - 1 to 0
            Level L = array.get(levels, i)
            if L.isBuy == isBuy and not L.traded and math.abs(lvl - L.price) <= tol
                L.touches := L.touches + 1
                merged := true
                break
    merged

f_newLevel(float lvl, bool isBuy, int bornBar, bool bornTraded, bool major) =>
    col = isBuy ? colBuy : colSell
    Level L = Level.new(price = lvl, born = bornBar, died = bar_index, touches = 1,
         isBuy = isBuy, isMajor = major, traded = bornTraded,
         ln = line.new(bornBar, lvl, bar_index, lvl, color = col,
                  width = liqWidth, extend = extend.right),
         lb = label.new(bar_index + 6, lvl, "", style = label.style_none,
                  textcolor = col, size = size.small),
         pEnt = line.new(bar_index, lvl, bar_index + 3, lvl, color = COL_NONE,
                  style = line.style_dotted, width = 1),
         pSl  = line.new(bar_index, lvl, bar_index + 3, lvl, color = COL_NONE,
                  style = line.style_dashed, width = 1),
         pTp  = line.new(bar_index, lvl, bar_index + 3, lvl, color = COL_NONE,
                  style = line.style_dashed, width = 1),
         pLb  = label.new(bar_index + 6, lvl, "", style = label.style_none,
                  textcolor = col, size = size.tiny))
    array.push(levels, L)
    // Cap PER SIDE. A combined cap looks equivalent and is not: in a one-sided
    // move only one side keeps producing swings, so a global trim evicts the
    // other side's levels one at a time until it has none left.
    int n = 0
    if array.size(levels) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(levels) - 1
            if array.get(levels, i).isBuy == isBuy
                n := n + 1
    if n > maxLevels
        if array.size(levels) > 0
            for i = 0 to array.size(levels) - 1
                Level q = array.get(levels, i)
                if q.isBuy == isBuy
                    f_killLevel(q)
                    array.remove(levels, i)
                    break

// Swing size decides MAJOR vs plain, and (optionally) whether to draw at all.
minMove     = close * majorPct / 100.0
swingUpSize = ph - ta.lowest(low, swingLen * 2 + 1)
swingDnSize = ta.highest(high, swingLen * 2 + 1) - pl

if not na(ph)
    major = swingUpSize >= minMove
    if major or not majorOnly
        if not f_absorb(ph, true)
            f_newLevel(ph, true, bar_index - swingLen, hiLag > ph, major)

if not na(pl)
    major = swingDnSize >= minMove
    if major or not majorOnly
        if not f_absorb(pl, false)
            f_newLevel(pl, false, bar_index - swingLen, loLag < pl, major)

// State every bar. Eviction is state too - deferring it to the last bar would
// leave dead levels competing for slots in the cap, so live ones get dropped.
bool sweptUp = false
bool sweptDn = false
if array.size(levels) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(levels) - 1
        Level L = array.get(levels, i)
        if not L.traded
            if L.isBuy and high > L.price
                L.traded := true
                L.died   := bar_index
                sweptDn := sweptDn or close < L.price
            if not L.isBuy and low < L.price
                L.traded := true
                L.died   := bar_index
                sweptUp := sweptUp or close > L.price

if array.size(levels) > 0 and not keepTraded
    for i = array.size(levels) - 1 to 0
        Level L = array.get(levels, i)
        if L.traded
            f_killLevel(L)
            array.remove(levels, i)

// Cosmetics on the last bar only. Redrawing every level on every historical bar
// is what froze the tab the last time this was built.
if barstate.islast and array.size(levels) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(levels) - 1
        f_drawLevel(array.get(levels, i))

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ④ FAIR VALUE GAPS + CE
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  A three-bar gap price skipped through. CE - consequent encroachment - is its
//  50% line, and is what most people actually treat as the level.
showFvgUp = input.bool(true, "Up gaps",   group = gFVG, inline = "f1")
showFvgDn = input.bool(true, "Down gaps", group = gFVG, inline = "f1")
colFvgUp  = input.color(color.new(#B22833, 80), "", group = gFVG, inline = "f1")
colFvgDn  = input.color(color.new(#089981, 71), "", group = gFVG, inline = "f1")
showCE    = input.bool(true, "Show CE (50%)", group = gFVG, inline = "f2")
colCE     = input.color(#B8B8B8, "", group = gFVG, inline = "f2")
useBodies = input.bool(true, "Measure with candle bodies, not wicks", group = gFVG,
     tooltip = "Body-to-body is the stricter reading: it counts only the range " +
               "where business was actually done, not where a wick reached.")
useCEfill = input.bool(false, "Mitigate at CE (rather than full fill)", group = gFVG)
delFilled = input.bool(true, "Delete filled gaps", group = gFVG)
maxFvg    = input.int(20, "Max gaps kept", minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = gFVG)

type Gap
    float top
    float bot
    bool  isUp
    box   bx
    line  ce

var array<Gap> gaps = array.new<Gap>()

// With bodies, the gap edges are the candle bodies two bars apart; with wicks,
// the high/low. The middle bar is the one that did the travelling.
upTop  = useBodies ? math.min(open, close)       : low
upBot  = useBodies ? math.max(open[2], close[2]) : high[2]
dnTop  = useBodies ? math.min(open[2], close[2]) : low[2]
dnBot  = useBodies ? math.max(open, close)       : high

isUpGap = upTop > upBot
isDnGap = dnTop > dnBot

f_newGap(float t, float b, bool up) =>
    col = up ? colFvgUp : colFvgDn
    Gap g = Gap.new(top = t, bot = b, isUp = up,
         bx = box.new(bar_index - 2, t, bar_index, b, border_color = COL_NONE,
                  bgcolor = col, extend = extend.right),
         ce = line.new(bar_index - 2, (t + b) / 2.0, bar_index, (t + b) / 2.0,
                  color = showCE ? colCE : COL_NONE, style = line.style_dotted,
                  extend = extend.right))
    array.push(gaps, g)
    while array.size(gaps) > maxFvg
        Gap dead = array.shift(gaps)
        box.delete(dead.bx)
        line.delete(dead.ce)

if showFvgUp and isUpGap
    f_newGap(upTop, upBot, true)
if showFvgDn and isDnGap
    f_newGap(dnTop, dnBot, false)

// Mitigation. An up gap dies when price comes back down through its level; a
// down gap when price comes back up through it.
if array.size(gaps) > 0
    for i = array.size(gaps) - 1 to 0
        Gap g = array.get(gaps, i)
        mid   = (g.top + g.bot) / 2.0
        probe = useBodies ? (g.isUp ? math.min(open, close) : math.max(open, close))
                          : (g.isUp ? low : high)
        target = useCEfill ? mid : (g.isUp ? g.bot : g.top)
        filled = g.isUp ? probe <= target : probe >= target
        if filled
            if delFilled
                box.delete(g.bx)
                line.delete(g.ce)
                array.remove(gaps, i)
            else
                box.set_right(g.bx, bar_index)
                box.set_extend(g.bx, extend.none)
                line.set_x2(g.ce, bar_index)
                line.set_extend(g.ce, extend.none)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ⑥ MEAN REVERSION  —  your measured engine
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Range fade + liquidity sweep: the pair that measured +0.368R at 57% green,
//  against -0.093R for trend pullback and -0.076R for breakout. Present so the
//  two philosophies can be compared on one chart rather than argued about.
bbLen   = input.int(20, "Bollinger length", minval = 2, maxval = 200, group = gMR)
bbMult  = input.float(2.0, "Bollinger mult", minval = 0.5, step = 0.1, group = gMR)
rangeLk = input.int(75, "Range lookback", minval = 5, maxval = 500, group = gMR)
minConf = input.int(2, "Confirmations required", minval = 1, maxval = 5, group = gMR,
     tooltip = "Two, and raising it is usually a mistake: adding more routes to " +
               "the minimum let WEAKER setups through, taking expectancy from " +
               "+0.29R to +0.06R when it was measured.")
mrSess  = input.bool(true, "07:00-20:00 London only", group = gMR,
     tooltip = "Load-bearing. Trading around the clock cost 92% of the edge.")

basis = ta.sma(close, bbLen)
dev   = bbMult * ta.stdev(close, bbLen)
hiN   = ta.highest(high, rangeLk)
loN   = ta.lowest(low,  rangeLk)
rsi   = ta.rsi(close, 14)

failUp = high > basis + dev and close < basis + dev and high >= hiN - 0.25 * atr
failDn = low  < basis - dev and close > basis - dev and low  <= loN + 0.25 * atr

barRng     = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
bullEngulf = close > open and close[1] < open[1] and close >= open[1] and open <= close[1]
bearEngulf = close < open and close[1] > open[1] and close <= open[1] and open >= close[1]
bullPin    = (math.min(open, close) - low) / barRng > 0.5
bearPin    = (high - math.max(open, close)) / barRng > 0.5

confLong  = (failDn ? 1 : 0) + (sweptUp ? 1 : 0) + (bullEngulf ? 1 : 0) +
             (bullPin ? 1 : 0) + (ta.crossover(rsi, 50) ? 1 : 0)
confShort = (failUp ? 1 : 0) + (sweptDn ? 1 : 0) + (bearEngulf ? 1 : 0) +
             (bearPin ? 1 : 0) + (ta.crossunder(rsi, 50) ? 1 : 0)

lonHour = hour(time, "Europe/London")
inSess  = not mrSess or (lonHour >= 7 and lonHour < 20)
tie     = confLong >= minConf and confShort >= minConf

mrLong  = useMR and inSess and (failDn or sweptUp) and confLong  >= minConf and not tie
mrShort = useMR and inSess and (failUp or sweptDn) and confShort >= minConf and not tie

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  MARKERS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
var array<label> sigLbls = array.new<label>()

f_pushLbl(label l) =>
    array.push(sigLbls, l)
    while array.size(sigLbls) > maxSigLbl
        label.delete(array.shift(sigLbls))

if showLbl and rfLong
    f_pushLbl(label.new(bar_index, low, "Long", style = label.style_label_up,
         color = #00E676, textcolor = #363A45, size = size.small, yloc = yloc.belowbar))
if showLbl and rfShort
    f_pushLbl(label.new(bar_index, high, "Short", style = label.style_label_down,
         color = #F23645, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small, yloc = yloc.abovebar))
if showLbl and mrLong
    f_pushLbl(label.new(bar_index, low, "MR ▲", style = label.style_label_up,
         color = color.new(#00E676, 35), textcolor = #363A45, size = size.tiny,
         yloc = yloc.belowbar))
if showLbl and mrShort
    f_pushLbl(label.new(bar_index, high, "MR ▼", style = label.style_label_down,
         color = color.new(#F23645, 35), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny,
         yloc = yloc.abovebar))

anyLong  = rfLong  or mrLong
anyShort = rfShort or mrShort
bgcolor(showBand and anyLong ? color.new(#00E676, 88) :
     showBand and anyShort ? color.new(#F23645, 88) : na, title = "Signal band")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  ALERTS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
alertcondition(rfLong,  "Range filter LONG",  "XAU-LE long")
alertcondition(rfShort, "Range filter SHORT", "XAU-LE short")
alertcondition(mrLong,  "Mean reversion LONG",  "XAU-LE MR long")
alertcondition(mrShort, "Mean reversion SHORT", "XAU-LE MR short")

if anyLong
    alert("XAU-LE LONG " + syminfo.ticker + " @ " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick),
         alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if anyShort
    alert("XAU-LE SHORT " + syminfo.ticker + " @ " + str.tostring(close, format.mintick),
         alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
